🛠️ How to handle dynamic elements in Appium
1. Use stable locators (BEST solution)

Prefer:

Accessibility ID
Resource ID

Instead of:

XPath
Index-based locators
Example:
driver.findElement(By.id("com.app:id/login_button"));

👉 Works even if UI layout changes

2. Use partial matching (text-based handling)

When text changes slightly:

Android UIAutomator2:

Powered by UIAutomator2

driver.findElement(MobileBy.AndroidUIAutomator(
    "new UiSelector().textContains(\"Login\")"
));

or:

textStartsWith("Log")
3. Use XPath with contains() (when needed)
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//*[contains(@text,'Login')]"));

👉 Useful for partially dynamic text

⚠️ Still slower than ID-based locators

4. Handle dynamic IDs using patterns

Sometimes IDs change like:

btn_login_12345
btn_login_67890
Solution:
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//*[starts-with(@resource-id,'btn_login')]"));
5. Use explicit waits (VERY important)

Dynamic elements often appear with delay.

WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(
    By.id("login_button")
));

👉 Prevents “element not found” errors due to timing issues

6. Use dynamic lists handling


Accessibility ID
🧠 What it is

Accessibility ID is a unique identifier given to an element for accessibility purposes (screen readers) and automation.

Android → content-desc
iOS → accessibilityIdentifier (used with XCUITest)
💻 Example
driver.findElement(MobileBy.AccessibilityId("login_button")).click();
👍 Advantages
Very fast
Highly stable
Cross-platform (Android + iOS)
Not affected by UI layout changes
👎 Disadvantages
Must be added by developers
Not always available in all apps
🔍 2. XPath
🧠 What it is

XPath is a query language used to navigate the UI hierarchy and locate elements based on structure or attributes.

💻 Example
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//android.widget.Button[@text='Login']"));

🧠 What is a UIAutomator locator?

A UIAutomator locator is an Android-specific way of finding elements using the UIAutomator2 engine.

Instead of relying on XPath or simple IDs, it uses Android’s native selector API:

UiSelector() → a Java-style query language for Android UI elements

It is mainly used in Appium for advanced Android element locating.

📱 Example of UIAutomator locator
driver.findElement(MobileBy.AndroidUIAutomator(
    "new UiSelector().text(\"Login\")"
));
🔍 What it means

This tells Android:

“Find an element whose text is Login”
⚙️ Common UIAutomator selectors

You can combine multiple conditions:

1. By text
new UiSelector().text("Login")
2. By resource ID
new UiSelector().resourceId("com.app:id/login_button")
3. By class name
new UiSelector().className("android.widget.Button")
4. By description (accessibility)
new UiSelector().description("login_button")
5. Combined conditions
new UiSelector()
    .className("android.widget.Button")
    .text("Login")
