🧠 Why tests become flaky

Common reasons:

1. Timing issues
Element not loaded yet
API response delay
2. Poor locators
XPath dependent on UI structure
Dynamic IDs
3. No proper waits
Using Thread.sleep()
4. UI animations / transitions
Element exists but not clickable yet
5. Device issues
Emulator lag
Network instability
6. Parallel execution conflicts
Shared driver or data issues
🛠️ How to handle flaky tests (real industry approach)
✅ 1. Use Explicit Waits properly (MOST IMPORTANT)

Instead of sleeps:

WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(
    By.id("login_button")
)).click();

👉 Used heavily in Appium frameworks

❌ Avoid this
Thread.sleep(5000);

👉 Causes unnecessary delays and still flaky

✅ 2. Fix locators (reduce XPath usage)

Prefer:

Accessibility ID
Resource ID

Bad:

By.xpath("//android.widget.Button[1]");

Good:

By.id("com.app:id/login_button");
✅ 3. Add retry mechanism for failed actions
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    try {
        driver.findElement(By.id("login")).click();
        break;
    } catch (Exception e) {
        Thread.sleep(2000);
    }
}

👉 Used for unstable UI elements

✅ 4. Use Fluent Wait (advanced control)
Wait<WebDriver> wait = new FluentWait<>(driver)
    .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(15))
    .pollingEvery(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
    .ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class);

👉 Useful for dynamic apps

✅ 5. Stabilize test data

Problems:

Same user used in multiple tests
Data already exists or missing

Solution:

Create fresh test data per run
Use APIs to reset state
✅ 6. Handle animations and transitions

Wait for stability:

wait.until(ExpectedConditions.invisibilityOf(loader));
✅ 7. Improve parallel execution safety

Use:

ThreadLocal driver
No shared static variables
private static ThreadLocal<AppiumDriver> driver = new ThreadLocal<>();
✅ 8. Retry failed tests at framework level

TestNG retry mechanism:

public class RetryAnalyzer implements IRetryAnalyzer {

    int count = 0;
    int maxRetry = 2;

    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {
        if (count < maxRetry) {
            count++;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
✅ 9. Improve app stability awareness

Sometimes issue is NOT automation:

Bug in app
Slow API
Device lag

👉 Don’t blindly blame automation

✅ 10. Add logging + screenshots

Capture failure evidence:

Screenshot on failure
Logs (Log4j / SLF4J)
Device logs (ADB)

1. “Session Not Created” Error
🧠 Meaning

Appium failed to start a session between your test and the device.

❌ Common causes
1. Wrong capabilities
Missing platformName
Wrong deviceName or udid
2. App not installed / invalid path
APK/IPA path incorrect
3. Appium server not running
Server not started or wrong port
4. Driver mismatch
Using wrong automation engine (Android/iOS)

Example Android uses UIAutomator2

🛠️ Fix
✅ Correct capabilities example
caps.setCapability("platformName", "Android");
caps.setCapability("automationName", "UIAutomator2");
caps.setCapability("deviceName", "emulator-5554");
caps.setCapability("app", "/path/app.apk");
✅ Ensure Appium server is running
appium -p 4723
✅ Check device connection
adb devices
🚨 2. “Element Not Found” (NoSuchElementException)
🧠 Meaning

Appium cannot locate the element in the current screen.

❌ Common causes
1. Element not loaded yet
Timing issue
2. Wrong locator
XPath incorrect
ID changed
3. Element inside WebView
Need context switch
4. Screen not in correct state
Navigation not completed
🛠️ Fix
✅ Use explicit wait (MOST IMPORTANT)
WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(
    By.id("login_button")
));
✅ Use better locators

Prefer:

Accessibility ID
Resource ID

Avoid:

By.xpath("//android.widget.Button[1]");
✅ Handle WebView context
driver.context("WEBVIEW");
✅ Verify screen state first

Make sure app is on correct page before locating element.

🚨 3. “Stale Element Reference Exception”
🧠 Meaning

Element was found earlier, but UI changed after that, so reference is no longer valid.

❌ Common causes
1. Page refresh / reload
2. Dynamic UI update
3. Element replaced after API response
🛠️ Fix
✅ 1. Re-locate element every time

❌ Wrong:

WebElement btn = driver.findElement(By.id("login"));
btn.click();
btn.click(); // stale error likely

✅ Correct:

driver.findElement(By.id("login")).click();
driver.findElement(By.id("login")).click();
✅ 2. Use wait after UI update
wait.until(ExpectedConditions.refreshed(
    ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id("login"))
));
✅ 3. Handle dynamic UI properly

If UI updates frequently:

Avoid storing WebElement in variable
Always re-fetch element
