Handling runtime permissions (Camera, Location, Storage, Notifications, etc.) is a very common requirement in mobile automation using Appium, especially on Android.

Apps usually show system popups like:

“Allow this app to access camera?”

If you don’t handle them properly, your test will fail before reaching the actual screen.

📱 1. Types of permission handling
1. Auto-grant via capabilities (BEST for Android)

You can tell Appium to automatically grant permissions at install time.

Example:
caps.setCapability("autoGrantPermissions", true);

👉 This is the easiest and most stable approach.

It works with UIAutomator2.

2. Grant permissions via ADB (pre-install method)

You can manually grant permissions before test execution:

adb shell pm grant com.app.package android.permission.CAMERA
adb shell pm grant com.app.package android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION
3. Handle permission popup in test (UI automation)

Sometimes you must click “Allow” buttons.

Example:
driver.findElement(By.id("com.android.permissioncontroller:id/permission_allow_button")).click();
Or multiple permissions:
driver.findElement(By.id("permission_allow_button")).click();
driver.findElement(By.id("permission_allow_button")).click();
4. Use UiAutomator (more stable Android way)
driver.findElement(MobileBy.AndroidUIAutomator(
    "new UiSelector().text(\"Allow\")"
)).click();
5. Deny permissions when needed
driver.findElement(MobileBy.AndroidUIAutomator(
    "new UiSelector().text(\"Deny\")"
)).click();

